; The parent names one server, the child another.  Here is the parent's
; half: it delegates example. to ns-parent.example., and carries the
; glue for that name because nothing else could.
;
; Nothing in this half is wrong, and nothing in the child's half is
; either.  What the scenario is about is that the two halves do not
; agree, which is a thing the protocol nowhere forbids and which some
; resolvers have made SERVFAIL of.
$ORIGIN .
$TTL 3600
@			IN	SOA	ns.root. root.root. 1 3600 900 604800 3600
@			IN	NS	ns.root.
ns.root.		IN	A	{{ROOT_ADDR}}
example.		IN	NS	ns-parent.example.
ns-parent.example.	IN	{{AUTH_ADDR_RR}}
example.		IN	DS	{{DS example}}
